{
  "result": "At the end of our street stood a small library that nobody seemed to notice until the rain came. Then its windows glowed like lanterns, and strangers gathered beneath its awning, pretending they had always intended to spend the afternoon among books. The librarian never corrected them. She simply smiled.\n\nInside, the building smelled of paper, lemon polish, and the faint dust of distant places. Maps curled on the walls. A clock above the desk ran seven minutes slow, which meant that everyone who entered was briefly granted more time than the rest of the town believed they possessed outside.\n\nOne winter, a boy began leaving notes inside books he loved. They were not reviews, exactly. One said, \u201cThis story feels like walking home with someone who understands you.\u201d Another read, \u201cPlease give the dragon a chance.\u201d Soon other readers answered him, and the shelves became a conversation between strangers.\n\nBy spring, the librarian had found dozens of messages. She could have removed them; rules were rules, after all. Instead, she placed a box of blank cards beside the clock. On the box she wrote, \u201cFor thoughts worth passing along.\u201d The cards disappeared almost as quickly as she replenished them.\n\nYears later, people remembered the library differently. Some recalled its stories, others its shelter, and a few its generous clock. But those who discovered the hidden notes remembered something else: the comforting evidence that a person they had never met had once felt exactly as they did in that moment.",
